<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>gravitation</title></head><body><h1>gravitation</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Newton's Law of Gravitation</li><li>Newton's Law of Gravitation</li><li>Gravitational Field Strength</li><li>Gravitational Potential and Potential Energy</li><li>Gravitational Potential and Potential Energy</li><li>Gravitational Potential; Gravitational Potential Energy</li><li>Variation in g due to Altitude and Depth</li><li>Variation in Value of g due to Altitude and Depth</li><li>Variation of g Due to Altitude and Depth</li><li>Centre of Mass and Centre of Gravity</li><li>Motion of a satellite: orbital velocity and time period o…</li><li>Motion of a Satellite — Orbital Velocity and Time Period</li><li>Motion of a Satellite</li><li>Escape Velocity</li><li>Geostationary Satellite</li><li>Potential and Kinetic Energy of the Satellite</li><li>GPS</li></ul><h2>Newton's Law of Gravitation</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Law statement:** every particle in the universe attracts every other particle with a force that is proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres: $F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**The universal constant G:** $G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N m}^2/\text{kg}^2$. It is the same everywhere in the universe (hence 'universal') and was first measured by Cavendish (1798) using a torsion balance. G is very small, which is why the gravitational attraction between everyday objects is imperceptible.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Vector form & direction:** the force on mass m₁ due to m₂ is $F_{12} = -G\frac{m_1m_2}{r^2} \hat{r}_{12}$, directed along the line joining the centres, always attractive. Newton's third law applies: $F_{12} = -F_{21}$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Superposition:** when several masses act on one particle, the net force is the vector sum of the individual forces: $F_{\text{net}} = \sum F_i$. Each pair obeys the inverse-square law independently.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — Earth and Moon:** $m_{\text{Earth}} = 6\times 10^{24} \text{ kg}$, $m_{\text{Moon}} = 7.4\times 10^{22} \text{ kg}$, $r = 3.84\times 10^8 \text{ m}$. $F = \frac{6.67\times 10^{-11} \times 6\times 10^{24} \times 7.4\times 10^{22}}{(3.84\times 10^8)^2} = 2.0\times 10^{20} \text{ N}$ — enough to hold the Moon in orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Gravitational field strength:** $g = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{GM}{r^2}$, the force per unit mass at a distance r from mass M. On Earth's surface $g = 9.8 \text{ m/s}^2$, which gives Earth's mass: $M = \frac{gR^2}{G} \approx 5.97\times 10^{24} \text{ kg}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Why G is used in orbits:** equating gravitational force to centripetal force ($\frac{GMm}{r^2} = \frac{mv^2}{r}$) gives orbital velocity $v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}$ and period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}$ — Kepler's third law follows directly.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Important comparisons:** gravity is the weakest of the four fundamental forces — between two protons the gravitational force is $\sim 10^{-36}$ times the electric force — yet it dominates cosmology because mass adds up while electric charges cancel.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Law statement:** every particle in the universe attracts every other particle with a force that is proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres: F = Gm₁m₂/r².</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**The universal constant G:** G = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ N m²/kg². It is the same everywhere in the universe (hence 'universal') and was first measured by Cavendish (1798) using a torsion balance. G is very small, which is why the gravitational attraction between everyday objects is imperceptible.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Vector form & direction:** the force on mass m₁ due to m₂ is F₁₂ = −G(m₁m₂/r²) r̂₁₂, directed along the line joining the centres, always attractive. Newton's third law applies: F₁₂ = −F₂₁.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Superposition:** when several masses act on one particle, the net force is the vector sum of the individual forces: F_net = Σ Fᵢ. Each pair obeys the inverse-square law independently.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Worked example — Earth and Moon:** m_Earth = 6×10²⁴ kg, m_Moon = 7.4×10²² kg, r = 3.84×10⁸ m. F = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 6×10²⁴ × 7.4×10²² / (3.84×10⁸)² = 2.0×10²⁰ N — enough to hold the Moon in orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Gravitational field strength:** g = F/m = GM/r², the force per unit mass at a distance r from mass M. On Earth's surface g = 9.8 m/s², which gives Earth's mass: M = gR²/G ≈ 5.97×10²⁴ kg.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Why G is used in orbits:** equating gravitational force to centripetal force (GMm/r² = mv²/r) gives orbital velocity v = √(GM/r) and period T = 2π√(r³/GM) — Kepler's third law follows directly.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Important comparisons:** gravity is the weakest of the four fundamental forces — between two protons the gravitational force is ~10⁻³⁶ times the electric force — yet it dominates cosmology because mass adds up while electric charges cancel.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>$F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$ — force is always attractive, proportional to both masses, inverse-square in distance.</td></tr><tr><td>$G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N·m}^2/\text{kg}^2$ is universal and independent of environment.</td></tr><tr><td>$g = \frac{GM}{r^2}$ is the force per unit mass; on Earth's surface it is 9.8 m/s².</td></tr><tr><td>The law explains orbital motion, tides, and planetary orbits.</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity between everyday objects is negligible because G is tiny.</td></tr><tr><td>F = Gm₁m₂/r² — force is always attractive, proportional to both masses, inverse-square in distance.</td></tr><tr><td>G = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg² is universal and independent of environment.</td></tr><tr><td>g = GM/r² is the force per unit mass; on Earth's surface it is 9.8 m/s².</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'There is a force of gravity only near Earth.' ✅ Gravity acts between ANY two masses anywhere in the universe — it never switches off, it only weakens with the square of distance.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'G can change with temperature or medium.' ✅ G is a universal constant — independent of temperature, pressure, medium, or the objects involved.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The force depends on the square of the distance — it dies quickly.' ✅ It dies as 1/r² but never reaches zero; at double distance the force is a quarter, at ten times it is one-hundredth.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier objects fall faster because gravity pulls them harder.' ✅ Gravity pulls harder on heavier objects ($F = mg$), but their acceleration is the same: $a = F/m = g$, hence all bodies in free fall accelerate equally (ignoring air resistance).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Earth pulls the Moon but the Moon does not pull Earth.' ✅ The forces are equal and opposite (Newton's third law). Earth and Moon pull on each other with equal forces — the Moon's acceleration is larger because its mass is smaller.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Inside Earth gravity stops.' ✅ Inside a uniform sphere, g decreases linearly with distance from the centre, reaching zero at the centre — it is not zero except exactly at the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier objects fall faster because gravity pulls them harder.' ✅ Gravity pulls harder on heavier objects (F = mg), but their acceleration is the same: a = F/m = g, hence all bodies in free fall accelerate equally (ignoring air resistance).</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Newton's law of gravitation states that every mass attracts every other mass with $F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$. The universal constant $G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N·m}^2/\text{kg}^2$ couples mass and distance to force. The law gives the gravitational field $g = \frac{GM}{r^2}$, explains orbital motion ($v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}$, $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}$), and underpins all celestial mechanics. Newton's law of gravitation states that every mass attracts every other mass with F = Gm₁m₂/r². The universal constant G = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg² couples mass and distance to force. The law gives the gravitational field g = GM/r², explains orbital motion (v = √(GM/r), T = 2π√(r³/GM)), and underpins all celestial mechanics.</p><hr><h2>Newton's Law of Gravitation</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Law statement:** every particle in the universe attracts every other particle with a force that is proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres: $F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**The universal constant G:** $G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N m}^2/\text{kg}^2$. It is the same everywhere in the universe (hence 'universal') and was first measured by Cavendish (1798) using a torsion balance. G is very small, which is why the gravitational attraction between everyday objects is imperceptible.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Vector form & direction:** the force on mass m₁ due to m₂ is $F_{12} = -G\frac{m_1m_2}{r^2} \hat{r}_{12}$, directed along the line joining the centres, always attractive. Newton's third law applies: $F_{12} = -F_{21}$.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Superposition:** when several masses act on one particle, the net force is the vector sum of the individual forces: $F_{\text{net}} = \sum F_i$. Each pair obeys the inverse-square law independently.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — Earth and Moon:** $m_{\text{Earth}} = 6\times 10^{24} \text{ kg}$, $m_{\text{Moon}} = 7.4\times 10^{22} \text{ kg}$, $r = 3.84\times 10^8 \text{ m}$. $F = \frac{6.67\times 10^{-11} \times 6\times 10^{24} \times 7.4\times 10^{22}}{(3.84\times 10^8)^2} = 2.0\times 10^{20} \text{ N}$ — enough to hold the Moon in orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Gravitational field strength:** $g = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{GM}{r^2}$, the force per unit mass at a distance r from mass M. On Earth's surface $g = 9.8 \text{ m/s}^2$, which gives Earth's mass: $M = \frac{gR^2}{G} \approx 5.97\times 10^{24} \text{ kg}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Why G is used in orbits:** equating gravitational force to centripetal force ($\frac{GMm}{r^2} = \frac{mv^2}{r}$) gives orbital velocity $v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}$ and period $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}$ — Kepler's third law follows directly.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Important comparisons:** gravity is the weakest of the four fundamental forces — between two protons the gravitational force is $\sim 10^{-36}$ times the electric force — yet it dominates cosmology because mass adds up while electric charges cancel.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>$F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$ — force is always attractive, proportional to both masses, inverse-square in distance.</td></tr><tr><td>$G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N·m}^2/\text{kg}^2$ is universal and independent of environment.</td></tr><tr><td>$g = \frac{GM}{r^2}$ is the force per unit mass; on Earth's surface it is 9.8 m/s².</td></tr><tr><td>The law explains orbital motion, tides, and planetary orbits.</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity between everyday objects is negligible because G is tiny.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'There is a force of gravity only near Earth.' ✅ Gravity acts between ANY two masses anywhere in the universe — it never switches off, it only weakens with the square of distance.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'G can change with temperature or medium.' ✅ G is a universal constant — independent of temperature, pressure, medium, or the objects involved.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The force depends on the square of the distance — it dies quickly.' ✅ It dies as 1/r² but never reaches zero; at double distance the force is a quarter, at ten times it is one-hundredth.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier objects fall faster because gravity pulls them harder.' ✅ Gravity pulls harder on heavier objects ($F = mg$), but their acceleration is the same: $a = F/m = g$, hence all bodies in free fall accelerate equally (ignoring air resistance).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Earth pulls the Moon but the Moon does not pull Earth.' ✅ The forces are equal and opposite (Newton's third law). Earth and Moon pull on each other with equal forces — the Moon's acceleration is larger because its mass is smaller.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Inside Earth gravity stops.' ✅ Inside a uniform sphere, g decreases linearly with distance from the centre, reaching zero at the centre — it is not zero except exactly at the centre.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Newton's law of gravitation states that every mass attracts every other mass with $F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$. The universal constant $G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N·m}^2/\text{kg}^2$ couples mass and distance to force. The law gives the gravitational field $g = \frac{GM}{r^2}$, explains orbital motion ($v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}$, $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}$), and underpins all celestial mechanics.</p><hr><h2>Gravitational Field Strength</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Gravitational potential V:** the work done per unit mass in bringing a test mass from infinity to a point, without acceleration. For a point mass M: $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$. At infinity $V = 0$, and everywhere else V is negative because gravity does positive work as the mass approaches — the field does the work, so potential decreases.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Gravitational potential energy U:** the work done against gravity in assembling a system; for two masses separated by r: $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$. It is the energy of the whole system, not of one mass alone. U is negative because the system is bound — you must supply $GMm/r$ of energy to separate the masses to infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why the minus sign matters:** potential energy is defined with zero at infinity. Since the force is attractive, energy is released (and PE decreases) as masses come together. A satellite in orbit at radius r has $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ — its total mechanical energy is $E = K + U = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, which is negative, confirming a bound orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Near Earth's surface:** for small heights h above the surface, $U = mgh$. This is the constant-g approximation of $-\frac{GMm}{r}$: since $\Delta(1/r) \approx -h/R^2$, the change matches $mgh$. Use $mgh$ only for $h \ll R$; use $-\frac{GMm}{r}$ for large altitudes (satellites, rockets).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — escape energy:** to launch a mass m from Earth's surface (R) to infinity you must supply $GMm/R$. Numerically for m = 1 kg: $\frac{6.67\times 10^{-11}\times 5.97\times 10^{24}}{6.37\times 10^6} \approx 6.25\times 10^{7} \text{ J} \approx 62.5 \text{ MJ}$ per kg. This energy corresponds to the escape speed 11.2 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Potential vs potential energy vs field:** field $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$ (the force per unit mass, a vector); potential V is a scalar (J/kg); potential energy $U = mV$. The field is the negative gradient of the potential: $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$ radially.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Equipotential surfaces:** surfaces of constant V. Moving along an equipotential surface requires zero work; the field is always perpendicular to these surfaces. For a point mass, equipotentials are concentric spheres.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Superposition:** for multiple masses, $V = \sum\left(-\frac{GM_i}{r_i}\right)$ and $U = \sum\left(-\frac{GM_i m}{r_i}\right)$ — potentials add as scalars, which is much simpler than adding force vectors.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>$V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is negative, approaching zero at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>$U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the energy of the (bound) system, negative for attraction.</td></tr><tr><td>Near Earth's surface $U = mgh$ is the appropriate approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>Potential and field connect: $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$.</td></tr><tr><td>Total energy of a circular orbit is negative: $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential and potential energy are the same.' ✅ Potential V is work per unit mass (J/kg); potential energy $U = mV$ (J). V belongs to a point in the field; U belongs to the mass–field system.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Negative energy is unphysical.' ✅ Negative potential energy simply means the system is BOUND — energy must be added to separate it. It is a matter of the reference at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ '$U = mgh$ and $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ disagree.' ✅ They agree for small heights: $mgh$ is the CHANGE in $-\frac{GMm}{r}$ near the surface. Use the right one for the régime.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential is maximum at Earth's surface.' ✅ Potential is most negative at the surface ($-\frac{GM}{R}$) and rises toward zero with altitude — at infinity it reaches the (higher) value 0.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Doubling the distance doubles the potential energy.' ✅ $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ — doubling r halves the magnitude, so U becomes $-\frac{GMm}{2r}$ (less negative).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite far away has lots of potential energy.' ✅ A far-away satellite has potential energy near ZERO (least negative, but $U \approx 0$). It needs enormous kinetic energy (orbital speed) that comes from the launch energy.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gravitational potential $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface $U \approx mgh$. The field relates to the potential by $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$, and circular orbits have total energy $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, confirming their bound nature.</p><hr><h2>Gravitational Potential and Potential Energy</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Gravitational potential V:** the work done per unit mass in bringing a test mass from infinity to a point, without acceleration. For a point mass M: $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$. At infinity $V = 0$, and everywhere else V is negative because gravity does positive work as the mass approaches — the field does the work, so potential decreases.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Gravitational potential energy U:** the work done against gravity in assembling a system; for two masses separated by r: $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$. It is the energy of the whole system, not of one mass alone. U is negative because the system is bound — you must supply $GMm/r$ of energy to separate the masses to infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why the minus sign matters:** potential energy is defined with zero at infinity. Since the force is attractive, energy is released (and PE decreases) as masses come together. A satellite in orbit at radius r has $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ — its total mechanical energy is $E = K + U = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, which is negative, confirming a bound orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Near Earth's surface:** for small heights h above the surface, $U = mgh$. This is the constant-g approximation of $-\frac{GMm}{r}$: since $\Delta(1/r) \approx -h/R^2$, the change matches $mgh$. Use $mgh$ only for $h \ll R$; use $-\frac{GMm}{r}$ for large altitudes (satellites, rockets).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — escape energy:** to launch a mass m from Earth's surface (R) to infinity you must supply $GMm/R$. Numerically for m = 1 kg: $\frac{6.67\times 10^{-11}\times 5.97\times 10^{24}}{6.37\times 10^6} \approx 6.25\times 10^{7} \text{ J} \approx 62.5 \text{ MJ}$ per kg. This energy corresponds to the escape speed 11.2 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Potential vs potential energy vs field:** field $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$ (the force per unit mass, a vector); potential V is a scalar (J/kg); potential energy $U = mV$. The field is the negative gradient of the potential: $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$ radially.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Equipotential surfaces:** surfaces of constant V. Moving along an equipotential surface requires zero work; the field is always perpendicular to these surfaces. For a point mass, equipotentials are concentric spheres.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Superposition:** for multiple masses, $V = \sum\left(-\frac{GM_i}{r_i}\right)$ and $U = \sum\left(-\frac{GM_i m}{r_i}\right)$ — potentials add as scalars, which is much simpler than adding force vectors.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>$V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is negative, approaching zero at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>$U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the energy of the (bound) system, negative for attraction.</td></tr><tr><td>Near Earth's surface $U = mgh$ is the appropriate approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>Potential and field connect: $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$.</td></tr><tr><td>Total energy of a circular orbit is negative: $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential and potential energy are the same.' ✅ Potential V is work per unit mass (J/kg); potential energy $U = mV$ (J). V belongs to a point in the field; U belongs to the mass–field system.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Negative energy is unphysical.' ✅ Negative potential energy simply means the system is BOUND — energy must be added to separate it. It is a matter of the reference at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ '$U = mgh$ and $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ disagree.' ✅ They agree for small heights: $mgh$ is the CHANGE in $-\frac{GMm}{r}$ near the surface. Use the right one for the régime.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential is maximum at Earth's surface.' ✅ Potential is most negative at the surface ($-\frac{GM}{R}$) and rises toward zero with altitude — at infinity it reaches the (higher) value 0.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Doubling the distance doubles the potential energy.' ✅ $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ — doubling r halves the magnitude, so U becomes $-\frac{GMm}{2r}$ (less negative).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite far away has lots of potential energy.' ✅ A far-away satellite has potential energy near ZERO (least negative, but $U \approx 0$). It needs enormous kinetic energy (orbital speed) that comes from the launch energy.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gravitational potential $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface $U \approx mgh$. The field relates to the potential by $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$, and circular orbits have total energy $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, confirming their bound nature.</p><hr><h2>Gravitational Potential and Potential Energy</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Gravitational potential V:** the work done per unit mass in bringing a test mass from infinity to a point, without acceleration. For a point mass M: $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$. At infinity $V = 0$, and everywhere else V is negative because gravity does positive work as the mass approaches — the field does the work, so potential decreases.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Gravitational potential energy U:** the work done against gravity in assembling a system; for two masses separated by r: $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$. It is the energy of the whole system, not of one mass alone. U is negative because the system is bound — you must supply $GMm/r$ of energy to separate the masses to infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why the minus sign matters:** potential energy is defined with zero at infinity. Since the force is attractive, energy is released (and PE decreases) as masses come together. A satellite in orbit at radius r has $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ — its total mechanical energy is $E = K + U = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, which is negative, confirming a bound orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Near Earth's surface:** for small heights h above the surface, $U = mgh$. This is the constant-g approximation of $-\frac{GMm}{r}$: since $\Delta(1/r) \approx -h/R^2$, the change matches $mgh$. Use $mgh$ only for $h \ll R$; use $-\frac{GMm}{r}$ for large altitudes (satellites, rockets).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — escape energy:** to launch a mass m from Earth's surface (R) to infinity you must supply $GMm/R$. Numerically for m = 1 kg: $\frac{6.67\times 10^{-11}\times 5.97\times 10^{24}}{6.37\times 10^6} \approx 6.25\times 10^{7} \text{ J} \approx 62.5 \text{ MJ}$ per kg. This energy corresponds to the escape speed 11.2 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Potential vs potential energy vs field:** field $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$ (the force per unit mass, a vector); potential V is a scalar (J/kg); potential energy $U = mV$. The field is the negative gradient of the potential: $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$ radially.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Equipotential surfaces:** surfaces of constant V. Moving along an equipotential surface requires zero work; the field is always perpendicular to these surfaces. For a point mass, equipotentials are concentric spheres.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Superposition:** for multiple masses, $V = \sum\left(-\frac{GM_i}{r_i}\right)$ and $U = \sum\left(-\frac{GM_i m}{r_i}\right)$ — potentials add as scalars, which is much simpler than adding force vectors.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>$V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is negative, approaching zero at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>$U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the energy of the (bound) system, negative for attraction.</td></tr><tr><td>Near Earth's surface $U = mgh$ is the appropriate approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>Potential and field connect: $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$.</td></tr><tr><td>Total energy of a circular orbit is negative: $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential and potential energy are the same.' ✅ Potential V is work per unit mass (J/kg); potential energy $U = mV$ (J). V belongs to a point in the field; U belongs to the mass–field system.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Negative energy is unphysical.' ✅ Negative potential energy simply means the system is BOUND — energy must be added to separate it. It is a matter of the reference at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ '$U = mgh$ and $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ disagree.' ✅ They agree for small heights: $mgh$ is the CHANGE in $-\frac{GMm}{r}$ near the surface. Use the right one for the régime.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential is maximum at Earth's surface.' ✅ Potential is most negative at the surface ($-\frac{GM}{R}$) and rises toward zero with altitude — at infinity it reaches the (higher) value 0.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Doubling the distance doubles the potential energy.' ✅ $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ — doubling r halves the magnitude, so U becomes $-\frac{GMm}{2r}$ (less negative).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite far away has lots of potential energy.' ✅ A far-away satellite has potential energy near ZERO (least negative, but $U \approx 0$). It needs enormous kinetic energy (orbital speed) that comes from the launch energy.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gravitational potential $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface $U \approx mgh$. The field relates to the potential by $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$, and circular orbits have total energy $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, confirming their bound nature.</p><hr><h2>Gravitational Potential; Gravitational Potential Energy</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Gravitational Potential: Gravitational potential $V$ at a point is the work done per unit mass in bringing a test mass from infinity to that point: $V = W/m = -GM/r$. It is a scalar quantity with SI unit J/kg. Potential is zero at infinity (reference) and negative everywhere else.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Gravitational Potential Energy: Potential energy $U$ of mass $m$ at a point where potential is $V$: $U = mV = -GMm/r$. Near Earth's surface ($h \ll R$): $U = mgh$ (relative to surface). Change in PE: $\Delta U = mg\Delta h$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Work Done by Gravitational Force: Work done in moving mass $m$ from $r_1$ to $r_2$: $W = -\Delta U = GMm(1/r_2 - 1/r_1)$. Work is path-independent (conservative force).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Gravitational Field and Potential Relation: $\vec{g} = -\nabla V$ or $g = -dV/dr$ for radial field. Field points in direction of decreasing potential.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Potential Due to Spherical Shell: Outside ($r > R$): $V = -GM/r$. Inside ($r < R$): $V = -GM/R$ (constant, equal to surface value). Field inside is zero but potential is not zero.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Potential Due to Solid Sphere: Outside ($r > R$): $V = -GM/r$. Inside ($r \le R$): $V = -\frac{GM}{2R^3}(3R^2 - r^2)$. At center: $V = -3GM/2R$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Escape Velocity: Minimum speed to escape to infinity from surface: $v_\text{esc} = \sqrt{2GM/R} = \sqrt{2gR}$. For Earth: $\approx 11.2\text{ km/s}$. Derived from energy conservation: $\frac{1}{2}mv^2 - GMm/R = 0$.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Gravitational Potential is Scalar: Unlike field (vector), potential adds algebraically. Potential at a point due to multiple masses: $V = -\sum Gm_i/r_i$.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Gravitational potential $V = -GM/r$ (scalar, J/kg, zero at infinity).</td></tr><tr><td>Potential energy $U = -GMm/r$; near surface $U = mgh$.</td></tr><tr><td>Potential is scalar — add algebraically for multiple masses.</td></tr><tr><td>$\vec{g} = -\nabla V$ — field points toward decreasing potential.</td></tr><tr><td>Shell: potential constant inside, $-GM/R$; field zero inside.</td></tr><tr><td>Escape velocity $v_\text{esc} = \sqrt{2GM/R} \approx 11.2\text{ km/s}$ for Earth.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Confusing potential (V, J/kg) with potential energy (U, J): $U = mV$ — multiply by mass.</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking potential is always positive: Gravitational potential is negative (infinity = 0 reference).</td></tr><tr><td>Believing field inside shell is zero means potential is also zero: Field = 0 but potential = constant ($-GM/R$).</td></tr><tr><td>Using $mgh$ for large heights: Valid only for $h \ll R$; use $-GMm/r$ for space problems.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing escape velocity with orbital velocity: $v_\text{esc} = \sqrt{2}\,v_\text{orb}$ at same radius.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gravitational potential $V = -GM/r$ is work per unit mass from infinity; potential energy $U = mV = -GMm/r$ (or $mgh$ near surface). Potential is scalar and negative; field $\vec{g} = -\nabla V$ points toward lower potential. Escape velocity $v_\text{esc} = \sqrt{2GM/R}$ is the minimum speed to escape to infinity.</p><hr><h2>Variation in g due to Altitude and Depth</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**g above the surface (altitude):** at height h above Earth's surface, $g' = \frac{GM}{(R+h)^2} = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$. As h increases, g decreases. For small h ($h \ll R$), a binomial expansion gives $g' \approx g\left(1 - \frac{2h}{R}\right)$ — a useful approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**g below the surface (depth):** at depth d, only the mass inside the concentric sphere of radius ($R-d$) contributes: $g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$ — gravity DECREASES linearly with depth. At the centre ($d = R$), $g' = 0$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why they differ:** above the surface, whole-earth mass acts from a larger distance ($1/r^2$ law); below the surface, the shell above the point exerts zero net force (shell theorem) and only the inner sphere matters, whose mass scales as ($R-d$)^3 while distance scales as ($R-d$) — giving linear decrease.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Worked example — altitude:** at $h = R$ (one Earth radius up), $g' = g\left(\frac{R}{2R}\right)^2 = g/4 = 2.45 \text{ m/s}^2$. At $h = 100$ km, using the small-h formula, the fractional decrease is $2h/R = 2\times 100/6400 = 0.031$, so $g' \approx 9.5 \text{ m/s}^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — depth:** at $d = R/2$ (3200 km deep), $g' = g(1 - 1/2) = g/2 = 4.9 \text{ m/s}^2$. At $d = R$ (centre) → $g' = 0$ (weightlessness, though pressure is immense).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Graphs (NEB favourite):** $g$ vs $r$ rises linearly from 0 at the centre to g at the surface, then falls as $1/r^2$ beyond the surface. The maximum value of g on Earth is at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Other causes of variation:** (1) rotation of Earth: g reduced by $\omega^2 R \cos^2 \lambda$ ($\lambda$ = latitude), maximum at the equator; (2) shape: Earth is an oblate spheroid — g is larger at the poles ($\approx 9.832$) than the equator ($\approx 9.78$); (3) local density anomalies (mountains, mineral deposits) cause tiny local changes used in geophysics.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Altitude formula for satellites:** for an orbit at height h, effective $r = R + h$: $v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{R+h}}$ and the orbital g is $g' = \frac{GM}{(R+h)^2}$ — the same g formula used at that radius.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**g above the surface (altitude):** at height h above Earth's surface, g' = GM/(R+h)² = g(R/(R+h))². As h increases, g decreases. For small h (h ≪ R), a binomial expansion gives g' ≈ g(1 − 2h/R) — a useful approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**g below the surface (depth):** at depth d, only the mass inside the concentric sphere of radius (R−d) contributes: g' = g(1 − d/R) — gravity DECREASES linearly with depth. At the centre (d = R), g' = 0.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Why they differ:** above the surface, whole-earth mass acts from a larger distance (1/r² law); below the surface, the shell above the point exerts zero net force (shell theorem) and only the inner sphere matters, whose mass scales as (R−d)³ while distance scales as (R−d) — giving linear decrease.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Worked example — altitude:** at h = R (one Earth radius up), g' = g(R/2R)² = g/4 = 2.45 m/s². At h = 100 km, using the small-h formula, the fractional decrease is 2h/R = 2×100/6400 = 0.031, so g' ≈ 9.5 m/s².</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Worked example — depth:** at d = R/2 (3200 km deep), g' = g(1 − 1/2) = g/2 = 4.9 m/s². At d = R (centre) → g' = 0 (weightlessness, though pressure is immense).</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>**Graphs (NEB favourite):** g vs r rises linearly from 0 at the centre to g at the surface, then falls as 1/r² beyond the surface. The maximum value of g on Earth is at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>**Other causes of variation:** (1) rotation of Earth: g reduced by ω²R cos²λ (λ = latitude), maximum at the equator; (2) shape: Earth is an oblate spheroid — g is larger at the poles (≈9.832) than the equator (≈9.78); (3) local density anomalies (mountains, mineral deposits) cause tiny local changes used in geophysics.</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>**Altitude formula for satellites:** for an orbit at height h, effective r = R + h: v = √(GM/(R+h)) and the orbital g is g' = GM/(R+h)² — the same g formula used at that radius.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Above the surface: $g' = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$ — decreases as $\frac{1}{(R+h)^2}$.</td></tr><tr><td>Below the surface: $g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$ — decreases linearly to zero at the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>g is maximum at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>Rotation and oblateness also reduce g at the equator.</td></tr><tr><td>The $g$–$r$ graph: linear rise inside, $1/r^2$ fall outside.</td></tr><tr><td>Above the surface: g' = g(R/(R+h))² — decreases as 1/(R+h)².</td></tr><tr><td>Below the surface: g' = g(1 − d/R) — decreases linearly to zero at the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>The g–r graph: linear rise inside, 1/r² fall outside.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'g increases with altitude.' ✅ Above the surface g DEcreases as $\frac{1}{(R+h)^2}$; below the surface it also decreases (linearly). g is max at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'From height h, g falls by $h/R$.' ✅ The small-h approximation is $2h/R$ (from the square), not $h/R$. At $h = 100$ km, that is ≈ 3%.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Deep inside Earth, g keeps growing.' ✅ It decreases linearly: $g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$, zero at the centre. The shell above you pulls equally in all directions (zero net).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'g is the same at poles and equator.' ✅ Rotation and the oblate shape make g slightly larger at the poles (~0.5% difference).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'At altitude 6400 km, g is half of surface.' ✅ At $h = R$, $g' = g/4$ — the height doubles the distance, so the square halves twice.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Weightlessness in orbit means no gravity.' ✅ Astronauts at 400 km still feel ~88% of surface gravity — they float because they are in free fall around Earth.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'g increases with altitude.' ✅ Above the surface g DEcreases as 1/(R+h)²; below the surface it also decreases (linearly). g is max at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'From height h, g falls by h/R.' ✅ The small-h approximation is 2h/R (from the square), not h/R. At h = 100 km, that is ≈ 3%.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Deep inside Earth, g keeps growing.' ✅ It decreases linearly: g' = g(1 − d/R), zero at the centre. The shell above you pulls equally in all directions (zero net).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'At altitude 6400 km, g is half of surface.' ✅ At h = R, g' = g/4 — the height doubles the distance, so the square halves twice.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> g varies with altitude ($g' = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$), depth ($g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$), and latitude. Above the surface g falls as the inverse square of distance; below the surface the shell theorem makes it fall linearly to zero at the centre. The $g$–$r$ graph is a straight rise then a $1/r^2$ fall. g varies with altitude (g' = g(R/(R+h))²), depth (g' = g(1 − d/R)), and latitude. Above the surface g falls as the inverse square of distance; below the surface the shell theorem makes it fall linearly to zero at the centre. The g–r graph is a straight rise then a 1/r² fall.</p><hr><h2>Variation in Value of g due to Altitude and Depth</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Acceleration Due to Gravity: At Earth's surface, $g = GM/R^2 \approx 9.8\text{ m/s}^2$. It varies with location due to altitude, depth, Earth's rotation, and shape.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Variation with Altitude: At height $h$ above surface: $g_h = GM/(R+h)^2 = g(1+h/R)^{-2} \approx g(1 - 2h/R)$ for $h \ll R$. Gravity decreases with altitude — inverse square law.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Variation with Depth: At depth $d$ below surface (assuming uniform density): $g_d = g(1 - d/R) = g(R-d)/R$. Gravity decreases linearly with depth, becoming zero at Earth's center.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Comparison: For small $h$ and $d$, decrease at height $h$ ($\Delta g \approx -2gh/R$) is twice the decrease at depth $d$ ($\Delta g = -gd/R$). Gravity decreases faster with altitude than with depth.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Earth's Rotation: Effective gravity at latitude $\lambda$: $g' = g - \omega^2 R \cos^2\lambda$. Maximum at poles ($\lambda = 90^\circ$, no rotation effect), minimum at equator ($\lambda = 0^\circ$, maximum centrifugal reduction).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Earth's Shape: Earth is oblate (equatorial radius > polar radius). Since $g \propto 1/R^2$, $g$ is higher at poles (closer to center) and lower at equator.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Combined Effects: The measured $g$ varies from about $9.78\text{ m/s}^2$ at equator to $9.83\text{ m/s}^2$ at poles due to rotation + shape + altitude.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Gravity decreases with altitude: $g_h = g(R/(R+h))^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>Gravity decreases with depth: $g_d = g(1-d/R)$, zero at center.</td></tr><tr><td>For equal h and d, decrease at height is approximately twice decrease at depth.</td></tr><tr><td>Rotation reduces effective gravity most at equator, none at poles.</td></tr><tr><td>Earth's oblateness makes g higher at poles, lower at equator.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>Thinking g decreases at same rate with height and depth: Height decrease (~2h/R) is approximately twice depth decrease (d/R).</td></tr><tr><td>Believing g is zero at Earth's center because no mass below: It's zero because mass surrounds symmetrically — pulls cancel.</td></tr><tr><td>Ignoring Earth's rotation in g variation: Rotation contributes significantly to equator-pole difference.</td></tr><tr><td>Using depth formula without uniform density assumption: Real Earth's density varies, so actual g(d) is not linear.</td></tr><tr><td>Confusing g variation with altitude vs gravitational potential variation.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Acceleration due to gravity decreases with altitude ($g_h \approx g(1-2h/R)$) and depth ($g_d = g(1-d/R)$), becoming zero at Earth's center. Rotation and Earth's oblateness cause g to be maximum at poles and minimum at equator. For equal h and d, gravity decreases about twice as fast with altitude.</p><hr><h2>Variation of g Due to Altitude and Depth</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**g above the surface (altitude):** at height h above Earth's surface, $g' = \frac{GM}{(R+h)^2} = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$. As h increases, g decreases. For small h ($h \ll R$), a binomial expansion gives $g' \approx g\left(1 - \frac{2h}{R}\right)$ — a useful approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**g below the surface (depth):** at depth d, only the mass inside the concentric sphere of radius ($R-d$) contributes: $g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$ — gravity DECREASES linearly with depth. At the centre ($d = R$), $g' = 0$.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why they differ:** above the surface, whole-earth mass acts from a larger distance ($1/r^2$ law); below the surface, the shell above the point exerts zero net force (shell theorem) and only the inner sphere matters, whose mass scales as ($R-d$)^3 while distance scales as ($R-d$) — giving linear decrease.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Worked example — altitude:** at $h = R$ (one Earth radius up), $g' = g\left(\frac{R}{2R}\right)^2 = g/4 = 2.45 \text{ m/s}^2$. At $h = 100$ km, using the small-h formula, the fractional decrease is $2h/R = 2\times 100/6400 = 0.031$, so $g' \approx 9.5 \text{ m/s}^2$.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — depth:** at $d = R/2$ (3200 km deep), $g' = g(1 - 1/2) = g/2 = 4.9 \text{ m/s}^2$. At $d = R$ (centre) → $g' = 0$ (weightlessness, though pressure is immense).</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Graphs (NEB favourite):** $g$ vs $r$ rises linearly from 0 at the centre to g at the surface, then falls as $1/r^2$ beyond the surface. The maximum value of g on Earth is at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Other causes of variation:** (1) rotation of Earth: g reduced by $\omega^2 R \cos^2 \lambda$ ($\lambda$ = latitude), maximum at the equator; (2) shape: Earth is an oblate spheroid — g is larger at the poles ($\approx 9.832$) than the equator ($\approx 9.78$); (3) local density anomalies (mountains, mineral deposits) cause tiny local changes used in geophysics.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Altitude formula for satellites:** for an orbit at height h, effective $r = R + h$: $v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{R+h}}$ and the orbital g is $g' = \frac{GM}{(R+h)^2}$ — the same g formula used at that radius.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Above the surface: $g' = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$ — decreases as $\frac{1}{(R+h)^2}$.</td></tr><tr><td>Below the surface: $g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$ — decreases linearly to zero at the centre.</td></tr><tr><td>g is maximum at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>Rotation and oblateness also reduce g at the equator.</td></tr><tr><td>The $g$–$r$ graph: linear rise inside, $1/r^2$ fall outside.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'g increases with altitude.' ✅ Above the surface g DEcreases as $\frac{1}{(R+h)^2}$; below the surface it also decreases (linearly). g is max at the surface.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'From height h, g falls by $h/R$.' ✅ The small-h approximation is $2h/R$ (from the square), not $h/R$. At $h = 100$ km, that is ≈ 3%.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Deep inside Earth, g keeps growing.' ✅ It decreases linearly: $g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$, zero at the centre. The shell above you pulls equally in all directions (zero net).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'g is the same at poles and equator.' ✅ Rotation and the oblate shape make g slightly larger at the poles (~0.5% difference).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'At altitude 6400 km, g is half of surface.' ✅ At $h = R$, $g' = g/4$ — the height doubles the distance, so the square halves twice.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Weightlessness in orbit means no gravity.' ✅ Astronauts at 400 km still feel ~88% of surface gravity — they float because they are in free fall around Earth.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> g varies with altitude ($g' = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$), depth ($g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$), and latitude. Above the surface g falls as the inverse square of distance; below the surface the shell theorem makes it fall linearly to zero at the centre. The $g$–$r$ graph is a straight rise then a $1/r^2$ fall.</p><hr><h2>Centre of Mass and Centre of Gravity</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Centre of mass (COM):** the point where the entire mass of a body (or system) may be considered to be concentrated for translational (linear) motion analysis. Position: $R_{cm} = \frac{\Sigma m_i r_i}{\Sigma m_i}$. For continuous bodies: $R_{cm} = \frac{\int r dm}{M}$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Centre of gravity (CG):** the point where the entire weight of a body may be considered to act. In a uniform gravitational field, the CG coincides exactly with the COM. If the field is non-uniform (very large bodies, varying g), the CG may shift slightly relative to the COM.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why CG equals COM in uniform g:** weight $W = mg$ at each point, and since $g$ is the same everywhere, the torque balance condition that defines the CG reduces to the same weighted average that defines the COM. The two centres coincide.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Solved example — two masses:** masses 2 kg at x = 0 and 8 kg at x = 3 m. COM: $x_{cm} = \frac{2 \times 0 + 8 \times 3}{2+8} = \frac{24}{10} = 2.4 \text{ m}$. The COM is closer to the heavier mass.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Solved example — system of three:** 1 kg at (0,0), 2 kg at (2,0), 3 kg at (0,4). $x_{cm} = \frac{1 \times 0 + 2 \times 2 + 3 \times 0}{6} = \frac{4}{6} = 0.67 \text{ m}$; $y_{cm} = \frac{1 \times 0 + 2 \times 0 + 3 \times 4}{6} = \frac{12}{6} = 2 \text{ m}$. COM at (0.67, 2) m.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Properties:** (1) The COM of a uniform, symmetric body lies at its geometric centre. (2) The COM of two bodies lies on the line joining them. (3) For irregular bodies the COM can lie OUTSIDE the material (e.g., a ring, a boomerang, a bent L-shape). (4) The total linear momentum of a system equals $M \cdot V_{cm}$ — external forces alone can change $V_{cm}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Finding CG experimentally (NEB practical):** suspend the body from two different points; the two vertical lines through the suspension points intersect at the centre of gravity. Plumb lines are used — the intersection of the two suspension lines locates the CG.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Applications:** designing stable structures (ships, cars, tables) keeps the CG low and inside the base; balancing acts use alignment of the CG above the support; in projectiles, the COM follows a smooth parabola even if the object rotates; spacecraft alignment uses the COM.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Centre of mass (COM):** the point where the entire mass of a body (or system) may be considered to be concentrated for translational (linear) motion analysis. Position: R_cm = (Σ mᵢrᵢ)/(Σ mᵢ). For continuous bodies: R_cm = (∫r dm)/M.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Why CG equals COM in uniform g:** weight W = mg at each point, and since g is the same everywhere, the torque balance condition that defines the CG reduces to the same weighted average that defines the COM. The two centres coincide.</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Solved example — two masses:** masses 2 kg at x = 0 and 8 kg at x = 3 m. COM: x_cm = (2×0 + 8×3)/(2+8) = 24/10 = 2.4 m. The COM is closer to the heavier mass.</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>**Solved example — system of three:** 1 kg at (0,0), 2 kg at (2,0), 3 kg at (0,4). x_cm = (1×0 + 2×2 + 3×0)/6 = 4/6 = 0.67 m; y_cm = (1×0 + 2×0 + 3×4)/6 = 12/6 = 2 m. COM at (0.67, 2) m.</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>**Properties:** (1) The COM of a uniform, symmetric body lies at its geometric centre. (2) The COM of two bodies lies on the line joining them. (3) For irregular bodies the COM can lie OUTSIDE the material (e.g., a ring, a boomerang, a bent L-shape). (4) The total linear momentum of a system equals M·V_cm — external forces alone can change V_cm.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>COM is a mass-weighted average position; CG is where the total weight acts.</td></tr><tr><td>In a uniform g, COM and CG coincide.</td></tr><tr><td>For uniform symmetric bodies, COM is at the geometric centre.</td></tr><tr><td>COM can lie outside the material (ring, L-shape, boomerang).</td></tr><tr><td>The motion of the COM is governed only by external forces.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'COM and CG are always at the material centre.' ✅ Only for uniform, symmetric bodies. A ring's COM is at its empty centre; an L-shaped body has its COM outside its material.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'COM and CG are always the same point.' ✅ They coincide in a UNIFORM gravitational field. For very large objects in a non-uniform field, they differ slightly.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'COM is a force point.' ✅ COM is a mass-weighted average position — a geometric point for translational dynamics, not a force.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'To find the COM, only the largest mass matters.' ✅ All masses contribute proportionally: $R_{cm} = \frac{\Sigma m_i r_i}{\Sigma m_i}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The COM moves when the body spins.' ✅ The COM of an isolated system moves uniformly (or stays still); spinning happens about the COM.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The CG of an object must lie within the object.' ✅ It can lie outside the material — ring, hollow sphere, L-shape, boomerang.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'To find the COM, only the largest mass matters.' ✅ All masses contribute proportionally: R_cm = Σmᵢrᵢ/Σmᵢ.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The centre of mass is the mass-weighted average position of a body or system: $R_{cm} = \frac{\Sigma m_i r_i}{M}$; the centre of gravity is the point where total weight acts. They coincide in a uniform gravitational field. The COM may lie outside the material and its motion is controlled only by external forces — the key to rigid-body and stability analysis. The centre of mass is the mass-weighted average position of a body or system: R_cm = Σmᵢrᵢ/M; the centre of gravity is the point where total weight acts. They coincide in a uniform gravitational field. The COM may lie outside the material and its motion is controlled only by external forces — the key to rigid-body and stability analysis.</p><hr><h2>Motion of a satellite: orbital velocity and time period o…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Fact.** Newton: F = G m₁m₂/r²; g = GM/r²; at Earth's surface g = GM/R² ≈ 9.8 m/s².</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Fact.** Orbital velocity v = √(GM/r); time period T = 2π√(r³/GM); escape velocity v_esc = √(2GM/r) = √2·v_orbital (at same r).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** For a uniform sphere, g ∝ r inside and g = 0 at the centre; g decreases as 1/r² outside.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Orbital velocity (v₀):** a satellite stays in a circular orbit when the gravitational pull GMm/r² provides the required centripetal force mv²/r. Equating: GMm/r² = mv₀²/r → v₀ = √(GM/r). Near Earth's surface, v₀ = √(gR) ≈ 7.9 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Why v₀ matters:** too slow → the satellite spirals in; too fast → it escapes to a higher ellipse. The orbital speed depends only on the radius (GM/r), not on the satellite's mass — heavy and light satellites share the same orbit speed.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Time period (T):** T = circumference/velocity = 2πr/v₀ = 2π√(r³/GM) — Kepler's third law. Squaring: T² ∝ r³. A higher orbit has a longer period: the Moon (r ≈ 3.84×10⁸ m) takes ~27.3 days; the ISS (r ≈ 6.8×10⁶ m) takes ~92 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Energy of an orbiting satellite:** K = ½mv₀² = GMm/2r, U = −GMm/r, so E_total = −GMm/2r. Notice K = −U/2 and E = U/2 — a signature of circular orbits. To raise a satellite to a higher orbit you must ADD energy, but the KE actually decreases (higher orbit = slower speed).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Worked example — ISS orbit:** at height 400 km, r = 6.8×10⁶ m. v₀ = √(6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 5.97×10²⁴/6.8×10⁶) = √(5.86×10⁷) ≈ 7.66 km/s. T = 2π×6.8×10⁶/7.66×10³ ≈ 5570 s ≈ 93 min.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Worked example — geostationary orbit:** for T = 24 h, r = (GMT²/4π²)^(1/3) ≈ 4.22×10⁷ m ≈ 35,800 km above the equator with v₀ ≈ 3.08 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Low Earth vs geostationary comparison:** LEO (200–2000 km): v ≈ 7.8 km/s, T ≈ 90 min, covers Earth rapidly (imaging, ISS). Geostationary (35,800 km): v ≈ 3.07 km/s, T = 24 h, appears fixed over one spot (communications, weather).</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>**Orbit changes:** to transfer between circular orbits, a Hohmann ellipse uses two engine burns; near perigee/apogee speeds change by Δv = v₂ − v₁. Firing thrust ahead slows the satellite and lowers the orbit; firing backward raises it.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Motion of a satellite: orbital velocity</td></tr><tr><td>• Time period of the satellite</td></tr><tr><td>v₀ = √(GM/r) — orbital speed depends only on radius, not satellite mass.</td></tr><tr><td>T = 2π√(r³/GM) — Kepler's third law: T² ∝ r³.</td></tr><tr><td>Higher orbit → slower speed, longer period.</td></tr><tr><td>E_total = −GMm/2r: bound orbit, with K = −U/2.</td></tr><tr><td>Astronauts float because of free fall, not absence of gravity.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ newton: f = g m₁m₂/r²; g = gm/r²; at earth's surface g = gm/r² ≈ 9.8 m/s²..</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite must be above the atmosphere to feel no gravity.' ✅ A satellite feels gravity at every altitude — it is in continuous free fall. It never falls to Earth because its forward speed curves its fall into an orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Weightlessness means g = 0 on the ISS.' ✅ g at 400 km is ≈ 8.7 m/s² (89% of surface). Astronauts float because both they and the station accelerate toward Earth together (free fall).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier satellites orbit slower.' ✅ Orbital speed is independent of the satellite's mass: v₀ = √(GM/r).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Higher orbit → faster speed.' ✅ Higher orbit means SLOWER speed (v₀ = √(GM/r)) but a LONGER period. To reach a higher orbit you must speed up at launch; once there, the circular speed is lower.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'T is proportional to r.' ✅ T ∝ r^{3/2} — the period grows faster than the radius.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite's kinetic energy increases as it moves to a higher orbit.' ✅ KE = GMm/2r DECREASES with height; the added energy is stored as potential energy (+GmM·Δ(1/r)) making the total E = −GMm/2r less negative.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A satellite orbits when gravity supplies the centripetal force: v₀ = √(GM/r) and T = 2π√(r³/GM). Energy is K = GMm/2r, U = −GMm/r, E = −GMm/2r. Orbital speed is mass-independent; higher orbits are slower but longer-period. Weightlessness arises from continuous free fall.</p><hr><h2>Motion of a Satellite — Orbital Velocity and Time Period</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Orbital velocity (v₀):** a satellite stays in a circular orbit when the gravitational pull GMm/r² provides the required centripetal force mv²/r. Equating: GMm/r² = mv₀²/r → v₀ = √(GM/r). Near Earth's surface, v₀ = √(gR) ≈ 7.9 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why v₀ matters:** too slow → the satellite spirals in; too fast → it escapes to a higher ellipse. The orbital speed depends only on the radius (GM/r), not on the satellite's mass — heavy and light satellites share the same orbit speed.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Time period (T):** T = circumference/velocity = 2πr/v₀ = 2π√(r³/GM) — Kepler's third law. Squaring: T² ∝ r³. A higher orbit has a longer period: the Moon (r ≈ 3.84×10⁸ m) takes ~27.3 days; the ISS (r ≈ 6.8×10⁶ m) takes ~92 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Energy of an orbiting satellite:** K = ½mv₀² = GMm/2r, U = −GMm/r, so E_total = −GMm/2r. Notice K = −U/2 and E = U/2 — a signature of circular orbits. To raise a satellite to a higher orbit you must ADD energy, but the KE actually decreases (higher orbit = slower speed).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — ISS orbit:** at height 400 km, r = 6.8×10⁶ m. v₀ = √(6.67×10⁻¹¹ × 5.97×10²⁴/6.8×10⁶) = √(5.86×10⁷) ≈ 7.66 km/s. T = 2π×6.8×10⁶/7.66×10³ ≈ 5570 s ≈ 93 min.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — geostationary orbit:** for T = 24 h, r = (GMT²/4π²)^(1/3) ≈ 4.22×10⁷ m ≈ 35,800 km above the equator with v₀ ≈ 3.08 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Low Earth vs geostationary comparison:** LEO (200–2000 km): v ≈ 7.8 km/s, T ≈ 90 min, covers Earth rapidly (imaging, ISS). Geostationary (35,800 km): v ≈ 3.07 km/s, T = 24 h, appears fixed over one spot (communications, weather).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Orbit changes:** to transfer between circular orbits, a Hohmann ellipse uses two engine burns; near perigee/apogee speeds change by Δv = v₂ − v₁. Firing thrust ahead slows the satellite and lowers the orbit; firing backward raises it.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>v₀ = √(GM/r) — orbital speed depends only on radius, not satellite mass.</td></tr><tr><td>T = 2π√(r³/GM) — Kepler's third law: T² ∝ r³.</td></tr><tr><td>Higher orbit → slower speed, longer period.</td></tr><tr><td>E_total = −GMm/2r: bound orbit, with K = −U/2.</td></tr><tr><td>Astronauts float because of free fall, not absence of gravity.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite must be above the atmosphere to feel no gravity.' ✅ A satellite feels gravity at every altitude — it is in continuous free fall. It never falls to Earth because its forward speed curves its fall into an orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Weightlessness means g = 0 on the ISS.' ✅ g at 400 km is ≈ 8.7 m/s² (89% of surface). Astronauts float because both they and the station accelerate toward Earth together (free fall).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier satellites orbit slower.' ✅ Orbital speed is independent of the satellite's mass: v₀ = √(GM/r).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Higher orbit → faster speed.' ✅ Higher orbit means SLOWER speed (v₀ = √(GM/r)) but a LONGER period. To reach a higher orbit you must speed up at launch; once there, the circular speed is lower.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'T is proportional to r.' ✅ T ∝ r^{3/2} — the period grows faster than the radius.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite's kinetic energy increases as it moves to a higher orbit.' ✅ KE = GMm/2r DECREASES with height; the added energy is stored as potential energy (+GmM·Δ(1/r)) making the total E = −GMm/2r less negative.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A satellite orbits when gravity supplies the centripetal force: v₀ = √(GM/r) and T = 2π√(r³/GM). Energy is K = GMm/2r, U = −GMm/r, E = −GMm/2r. Orbital speed is mass-independent; higher orbits are slower but longer-period. Weightlessness arises from continuous free fall.</p><hr><h2>Motion of a Satellite</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Orbital velocity (v₀):** a satellite stays in a circular orbit when the gravitational pull $GMm/r^2$ provides the required centripetal force $mv^2/r$. Equating: $GMm/r^2 = mv_0^2/r \rightarrow v_0 = \sqrt{GM/r}$. Near Earth's surface, $v_0 = \sqrt{gR} \approx 7.9 \text{ km/s}$.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Why v₀ matters:** too slow → the satellite spirals in; too fast → it escapes to a higher ellipse. The orbital speed depends only on the radius (GM/r), not on the satellite's mass — heavy and light satellites share the same orbit speed.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Time period (T):** $T = \frac{2\pi r}{v_0} = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}$ — Kepler's third law. Squaring: $T^2 \propto r^3$. A higher orbit has a longer period: the Moon (r ≈ 3.84×10⁸ m) takes ~27.3 days; the ISS (r ≈ 6.8×10⁶ m) takes ~92 minutes.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Energy of an orbiting satellite:** $K = \frac{1}{2}mv_0^2 = \frac{GMm}{2r}$, $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$, so $E_{total} = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$. Notice $K = -U/2$ and $E = U/2$ — a signature of circular orbits. To raise a satellite to a higher orbit you must ADD energy, but the KE actually decreases (higher orbit = slower speed).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — ISS orbit:** at height 400 km, r = 6.8×10⁶ m. $v_0 = \sqrt{\frac{6.67\times10^{-11} \times 5.97\times10^{24}}{6.8\times10^6}} = \sqrt{5.86\times10^7} \approx 7.66 \text{ km/s}$. $T = \frac{2\pi \times 6.8\times10^6}{7.66\times10^3} \approx 5570 \text{ s} \approx 93 \text{ min}$.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Worked example — geostationary orbit:** for T = 24 h, $r = \left(\frac{GMT^2}{4\pi^2}\right)^{1/3} \approx 4.22\times10^7 \text{ m} \approx 35,800 \text{ km}$ above the equator with $v_0 \approx 3.08 \text{ km/s}$.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Low Earth vs geostationary comparison:** LEO (200–2000 km): v ≈ 7.8 km/s, T ≈ 90 min, covers Earth rapidly (imaging, ISS). Geostationary (35,800 km): v ≈ 3.07 km/s, T = 24 h, appears fixed over one spot (communications, weather).</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Orbit changes:** to transfer between circular orbits, a Hohmann ellipse uses two engine burns; near perigee/apogee speeds change by $\Delta v = v_2 - v_1$. Firing thrust ahead slows the satellite and lowers the orbit; firing backward raises it.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>$v_0 = \sqrt{GM/r}$ — orbital speed depends only on radius, not satellite mass.</td></tr><tr><td>$T = 2\pi\sqrt{r^3/GM}$ — Kepler's third law: $T^2 \propto r^3$.</td></tr><tr><td>Higher orbit → slower speed, longer period.</td></tr><tr><td>$E_{total} = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$: bound orbit, with $K = -U/2$.</td></tr><tr><td>Astronauts float because of free fall, not absence of gravity.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite must be above the atmosphere to feel no gravity.' ✅ A satellite feels gravity at every altitude — it is in continuous free fall. It never falls to Earth because its forward speed curves its fall into an orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Weightlessness means g = 0 on the ISS.' ✅ g at 400 km is ≈ 8.7 m/s² (89% of surface). Astronauts float because both they and the station accelerate toward Earth together (free fall).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Heavier satellites orbit slower.' ✅ Orbital speed is independent of the satellite's mass: $v_0 = \sqrt{GM/r}$.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Higher orbit → faster speed.' ✅ Higher orbit means SLOWER speed ($v_0 = \sqrt{GM/r}$) but a LONGER period. To reach a higher orbit you must speed up at launch; once there, the circular speed is lower.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'T is proportional to r.' ✅ $T \propto r^{3/2}$ — the period grows faster than the radius.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite's kinetic energy increases as it moves to a higher orbit.' ✅ $KE = \frac{GMm}{2r}$ DECREASES with height; the added energy is stored as potential energy ($+GmM\cdot\Delta(1/r)$) making the total $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$ less negative.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A satellite orbits when gravity supplies the centripetal force: $v_0 = \sqrt{GM/r}$ and $T = 2\pi\sqrt{r^3/GM}$. Energy is $K = \frac{GMm}{2r}$, $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$, $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$. Orbital speed is mass-independent; higher orbits are slower but longer-period. Weightlessness arises from continuous free fall.</p><hr><h2>Escape Velocity</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Escape velocity is the minimum speed at radius r that lets an object reach infinity with zero final speed when no further thrust or drag acts. With gravitational potential energy zero at infinity, energy conservation gives (1/2)mv²−GMm/r=0.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Solving gives ve=√(2GM/r). At the surface r=R, g=GM/R², so ve=√(2gR). The escaping object’s mass cancels, so the ideal threshold is independent of its mass.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Worked Earth estimate: g=9.81 m s⁻² and R=6.37×10⁶ m give ve=√(2gR)≈1.12×10⁴ m s⁻¹=11.2 km s⁻¹.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>At altitude h, use r=R+h: ve(h)/ve(0)=√[R/(R+h)]. At h=R, local escape speed is 1/√2 of its surface value.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Circular orbital speed at the same radius is vc=√(GM/r), so ve=√2 vc. This is an ideal energy threshold; real launches must account for atmosphere, rotation, gravity losses and propulsion.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>At escape threshold, total mechanical energy is zero at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>Use r=R+h for altitude, not the surface radius.</td></tr><tr><td>The orbital-speed relation compares both speeds at the same radius.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>The formula neglects air resistance, rotation, external bodies and continued thrust.</td></tr><tr><td>Escape speed is not the practical rocket delta-v from the launch pad.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Energy conservation gives ve=√(2GM/R)=√(2gR) for an ideal surface launch. It is independent of projectile mass and √2 times circular speed at the same radius.</p><hr><h2>Geostationary Satellite</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Geostationary satellite:** a satellite that appears stationary relative to a point on the Earth's equator. It must satisfy three conditions: (1) orbit in the plane of the equator (equatorial plane), (2) orbit in the same direction as Earth's rotation (west → east), and (3) have a period exactly equal to Earth's rotation period (T = 24 hours).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Height above Earth:** using T = 2π√(r³/GM) with T = 86400 s → r ≈ 4.22×10⁷ m from the centre → altitude h = r − R ≈ 35,800 km above the surface. This is about 5.6 Earth radii.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Orbital speed:** v₀ = 2πr/T = 2π×4.22×10⁷/86400 ≈ 3.07 km/s. It matches Earth's angular speed: the satellite and the point below rotate together, so the satellite hangs over the same longitude.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Only the equator works:** a satellite over the equator is the only way the orbit plane can contain both the satellite and Earth's rotation axis while keeping the satellite fixed over one point. An inclined orbit would carry the satellite north and south each day.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Why 24 h is exact:** for T = T_Earth, the angular speeds match: ω_sat = ω_Earth. From r³ = GMT²/4π² the altitude is fixed (~35,800 km) — there is a unique geostationary height.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Applications:** communications satellites (broadcast, TV, internet), weather satellites (HIMAWARI, INSAT type), and some navigation augmentation systems. Their fixed position means ground dishes never need to move.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Geostationary vs geosynchronous:** a geosynchronous satellite has a 24-h period but may have an inclined or elliptical orbit, so it drifts north/south in the sky. A geostationary satellite is a special geosynchronous satellite confined to the equatorial plane at fixed longitude.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Energy at geostationary orbit:** for m = 1000 kg, U = −GMm/r ≈ −9.4×10⁹ J, E_total = −GMm/2r ≈ −4.7×10⁹ J. Launching to this height needs a large boost — the standard launch uses a geostationary transfer orbit (GTO) followed by an apogee burn.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>A geostationary satellite orbits in the equatorial plane, west-to-east, with T = 24 h.</td></tr><tr><td>Its unique altitude is ≈ 35,800 km (r ≈ 4.22×10⁷ m).</td></tr><tr><td>v ≈ 3.07 km/s — matches Earth's rotation, so it appears fixed overhead.</td></tr><tr><td>Applications: communication, broadcasting, weather satellites.</td></tr><tr><td>Geosynchronous ≠ geostationary — geosynchronous only matches the period.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Any 24-hour satellite is geostationary.' ✅ A geostationary satellite must ALSO be equatorial, circular, and west-to-east. A geosynchronous one just matches the 24-h period.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Geostationary orbit can be at any height above the equator.' ✅ There is exactly ONE geostationary altitude: ≈ 35,800 km. It is set by Earth's rotation period.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A geostationary satellite stays above one spot because it has no gravity.' ✅ It stays because its orbital period exactly equals Earth's rotation; gravity still holds it in orbit at v ≈ 3.07 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The satellite is stationary in space.' ✅ It moves at 3.07 km/s; it is stationary only relative to the rotating Earth.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'At high latitudes people see it overhead.' ✅ A geostationary satellite is always over the equator — at high latitudes it appears low on the horizon, and near the poles it is not visible at all.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'The Moon is a geostationary satellite.' ✅ The Moon has a 27.3-day period and an inclined orbit — it is not geostationary.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> A geostationary satellite orbits in the equatorial plane west-to-east with a 24-hour period, so it appears fixed over one point on the equator. It sits at a unique altitude of 35,800 km (r = 4.22×10⁷ m) and moves at 3.07 km/s. Geostationary orbits are vital for communications and weather satellites.</p><hr><h2>Potential and Kinetic Energy of the Satellite</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Gravitational potential V:** the work done per unit mass in bringing a test mass from infinity to a point, without acceleration. For a point mass M: V = −GM/r. At infinity V = 0, and everywhere else V is negative because gravity does positive work as the mass approaches — the field does the work, so potential decreases.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Gravitational potential energy U:** the work done against gravity in assembling a system; for two masses separated by r: U = −GMm/r. It is the energy of the whole system, not of one mass alone. U is negative because the system is bound — you must supply GMm/r of energy to separate the masses to infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Why the minus sign matters:** potential energy is defined with zero at infinity. Since the force is attractive, energy is released (and PE decreases) as masses come together. A satellite in orbit at radius r has U = −GMm/r — its total mechanical energy is E = K + U = −GMm/2r, which is negative, confirming a bound orbit.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Near Earth's surface:** for small heights h above the surface, U = mgh. This is the constant-g approximation of −GMm/r: since Δ(1/r) ≈ −h/R², the change matches mgh. Use mgh only for h ≪ R; use −GMm/r for large altitudes (satellites, rockets).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Worked example — escape energy:** to launch a mass m from Earth's surface (R) to infinity you must supply GMm/R. Numerically for m = 1 kg: (6.67×10⁻¹¹)(5.97×10²⁴)/6.37×10⁶ ≈ 6.25×10⁷ J ≈ 62.5 MJ per kg. This energy corresponds to the escape speed 11.2 km/s.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Potential vs potential energy vs field:** field g = −dV/dr (the force per unit mass, a vector); potential V is a scalar (J/kg); potential energy U = mV. The field is the negative gradient of the potential: g = −dV/dr radially.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Equipotential surfaces:** surfaces of constant V. Moving along an equipotential surface requires zero work; the field is always perpendicular to these surfaces. For a point mass, equipotentials are concentric spheres.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Superposition:** for multiple masses, V = Σ(−GMᵢ/rᵢ) and U = Σ(−GMᵢm/rᵢ) — potentials add as scalars, which is much simpler than adding force vectors.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>V = −GM/r is negative, approaching zero at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>U = −GMm/r is the energy of the (bound) system, negative for attraction.</td></tr><tr><td>Near Earth's surface U = mgh is the appropriate approximation.</td></tr><tr><td>Potential and field connect: g = −dV/dr.</td></tr><tr><td>Total energy of a circular orbit is negative: E = −GMm/2r.</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential and potential energy are the same.' ✅ Potential V is work per unit mass (J/kg); potential energy U = mV (J). V belongs to a point in the field; U belongs to the mass–field system.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Negative energy is unphysical.' ✅ Negative potential energy simply means the system is BOUND — energy must be added to separate it. It is a matter of the reference at infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'U = mgh and U = −GMm/r disagree.' ✅ They agree for small heights: mgh is the CHANGE in −GMm/r near the surface. Use the right one for the régime.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Potential is maximum at Earth's surface.' ✅ Potential is most negative at the surface (−GM/R) and rises toward zero with altitude — at infinity it reaches the (higher) value 0.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Doubling the distance doubles the potential energy.' ✅ U = −GMm/r — doubling r halves the magnitude, so U becomes −GMm/2r (less negative).</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'A satellite far away has lots of potential energy.' ✅ A far-away satellite has potential energy near ZERO (least negative, but U ≈ 0). It needs enormous kinetic energy (orbital speed) that comes from the launch energy.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Gravitational potential V = −GM/r is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy U = −GMm/r is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface U ≈ mgh. The field relates to the potential by g = −dV/dr, and circular orbits have total energy E = −GMm/2r, confirming their bound nature.</p><hr><h2>GPS</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**What GPS is:** the Global Positioning System is a satellite-based navigation system that tells a receiver its exact 3D position on Earth. It uses the same gravitational/orbital physics as any satellite, which is why it lives in the gravitation chapter.</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Principle of position fixing:** each GPS satellite broadcasts a radio signal stamped with its position and precise transmission time. The receiver measures the travel time of signals from at least four satellites and solves for (x, y, z, t) — the fourth equation corrects the receiver clock offset.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**How distance is measured:** distance = speed of light × time delay. Since the receiver's clock is not atomic-precise, a fourth satellite provides the clock-error term, giving accuracy of a few metres.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Satellite constellation:** 24+ satellites orbit at ≈ 20,200 km altitude (medium Earth orbit) in six planes inclined 55° to the equator. Each satellite has a period of ≈ 11 h 58 min (half a sidereal day) and speed ≈ 3.87 km/s — from T = 2π√(r³/GM).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Why the orbits are chosen:** six planes × four satellites each guarantee that at least 4–6 satellites are above the horizon at any place and time. The 55° inclination covers up to the poles, unlike a geostationary belt.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Relativity in GPS:** satellite clocks run faster than ground clocks by ~38 µs/day (7 µs from special relativity + 45 µs from general relativity). This systematic shift must be compensated, otherwise GPS positions would drift by ~10 km per day — a practical proof of relativity.</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Accuracy and sources of error:** ionospheric delays, multipath reflections, satellite clocks, and geometry (DOP). With corrections (differential GPS / WAAS / dual-frequency) accuracy reaches centimetre level for surveying.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Applications:** navigation (cars, ships, aircraft), mapping, timing synchronisation for power grids and financial networks, agriculture, disaster relief, and scientific studies (tectonic plate motion). GPS is a direct practical payoff of satellite motion physics.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>GPS fixes position by measuring radio signal travel time from ≥ 4 satellites.</td></tr><tr><td>Four satellites allow solving the receiver clock error as a fourth unknown.</td></tr><tr><td>GPS satellites orbit at ≈ 20,200 km in inclined MEO with ≈ 12 h periods.</td></tr><tr><td>Relativistic corrections (~38 µs/day) are essential for metre-level accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>GPS relies directly on satellite motion physics (v = √(GM/r), T = 2π√(r³/GM)).</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ 'GPS signal carries your position from the satellite.' ✅ The satellite only sends its OWN position + time. YOUR receiver computes your position by triangulating delays.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Three satellites suffice for full 3D positioning.' ✅ Three give (x,y,z) only if the clock is perfect; a fourth is required for a practical unknown clock offset.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'GPS works by measuring signal strength.' ✅ It measures signal TRAVEL TIME (c × Δt), not strength.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'GPS satellites are geostationary.' ✅ They orbit at ≈ 20,200 km in inclined MEO orbits with ≈ 12 h periods — not geostationary.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Relativistic corrections are too tiny to matter.' ✅ Without them GPS would be off by ~10 km/day. The effect is 38 µs/day — essential.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ 'Position accuracy is limited by the number of satellites visible.' ✅ With ≥ 4 satellites you can fix position; more satellites reduce error statistics but don't change the basic math.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> The Global Positioning System uses ≥ 4 satellites whose positions and times are known to compute a receiver's (x, y, z, t) by measuring signal travel times (d = c·Δt). The constellation orbits at ≈ 20,200 km with ≈ 12 h periods, and relativistic clock corrections of ~38 µs/day are essential to maintain metre-level accuracy.</p><hr><h2>Comparison Tables</h2><h3>Concept Comparison</h3><table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr><tr><td>1: Newton's Law of Gravitation</td><td>Newton's law of gravitation states that every mass attracts every other mass with $F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$. The universal constant $G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N·m}^2/\text{kg}^2$ couples mass and distance to force. The law gives the gravitational field $g = \frac{GM}{r^2}$, explains orbital motion ($v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}$, $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}$), and underpins all celestial mechanics. Newton's law of gravitation states that every mass attracts every other mass with F = Gm₁m₂/r². The universal constant G = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ N·m²/kg² couples mass and distance to force. The law gives the gravitational field g = GM/r², explains orbital motion (v = √(GM/r), T = 2π√(r³/GM)), and underpins all celestial mechanics.</td></tr><tr><td>2: Newton's Law of Gravitation</td><td>Newton's law of gravitation states that every mass attracts every other mass with $F = \frac{Gm_1m_2}{r^2}$. The universal constant $G = 6.67\times 10^{-11} \text{ N·m}^2/\text{kg}^2$ couples mass and distance to force. The law gives the gravitational field $g = \frac{GM}{r^2}$, explains orbital motion ($v = \sqrt{\frac{GM}{r}}$, $T = 2\pi\sqrt{\frac{r^3}{GM}}$), and underpins all celestial mechanics.</td></tr><tr><td>3: Gravitational Field Strength</td><td>Gravitational potential $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface $U \approx mgh$. The field relates to the potential by $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$, and circular orbits have total energy $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, confirming their bound nature.</td></tr><tr><td>4: Gravitational Potential and Potential Energy</td><td>Gravitational potential $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface $U \approx mgh$. The field relates to the potential by $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$, and circular orbits have total energy $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, confirming their bound nature.</td></tr><tr><td>5: Gravitational Potential and Potential Energy</td><td>Gravitational potential $V = -\frac{GM}{r}$ is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$ is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface $U \approx mgh$. The field relates to the potential by $g = -\frac{dV}{dr}$, and circular orbits have total energy $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$, confirming their bound nature.</td></tr><tr><td>6: Gravitational Potential; Gravitational Potential Energy</td><td>Gravitational potential $V = -GM/r$ is work per unit mass from infinity; potential energy $U = mV = -GMm/r$ (or $mgh$ near surface). Potential is scalar and negative; field $\vec{g} = -\nabla V$ points toward lower potential. Escape velocity $v_\text{esc} = \sqrt{2GM/R}$ is the minimum speed to escape to infinity.</td></tr><tr><td>7: Variation in g due to Altitude and Depth</td><td>g varies with altitude ($g' = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$), depth ($g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$), and latitude. Above the surface g falls as the inverse square of distance; below the surface the shell theorem makes it fall linearly to zero at the centre. The $g$–$r$ graph is a straight rise then a $1/r^2$ fall. g varies with altitude (g' = g(R/(R+h))²), depth (g' = g(1 − d/R)), and latitude. Above the surface g falls as the inverse square of distance; below the surface the shell theorem makes it fall linearly to zero at the centre. The g–r graph is a straight rise then a 1/r² fall.</td></tr><tr><td>8: Variation in Value of g due to Altitude and Depth</td><td>Acceleration due to gravity decreases with altitude ($g_h \approx g(1-2h/R)$) and depth ($g_d = g(1-d/R)$), becoming zero at Earth's center. Rotation and Earth's oblateness cause g to be maximum at poles and minimum at equator. For equal h and d, gravity decreases about twice as fast with altitude.</td></tr><tr><td>9: Variation of g Due to Altitude and Depth</td><td>g varies with altitude ($g' = g\left(\frac{R}{R+h}\right)^2$), depth ($g' = g\left(1 - \frac{d}{R}\right)$), and latitude. Above the surface g falls as the inverse square of distance; below the surface the shell theorem makes it fall linearly to zero at the centre. The $g$–$r$ graph is a straight rise then a $1/r^2$ fall.</td></tr><tr><td>10: Centre of Mass and Centre of Gravity</td><td>The centre of mass is the mass-weighted average position of a body or system: $R_{cm} = \frac{\Sigma m_i r_i}{M}$; the centre of gravity is the point where total weight acts. They coincide in a uniform gravitational field. The COM may lie outside the material and its motion is controlled only by external forces — the key to rigid-body and stability analysis. The centre of mass is the mass-weighted average position of a body or system: R_cm = Σmᵢrᵢ/M; the centre of gravity is the point where total weight acts. They coincide in a uniform gravitational field. The COM may lie outside the material and its motion is controlled only by external forces — the key to rigid-body and stability analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>11: Motion of a satellite: orbital velocity and time period o…</td><td>A satellite orbits when gravity supplies the centripetal force: v₀ = √(GM/r) and T = 2π√(r³/GM). Energy is K = GMm/2r, U = −GMm/r, E = −GMm/2r. Orbital speed is mass-independent; higher orbits are slower but longer-period. Weightlessness arises from continuous free fall.</td></tr><tr><td>12: Motion of a Satellite — Orbital Velocity and Time Period</td><td>A satellite orbits when gravity supplies the centripetal force: v₀ = √(GM/r) and T = 2π√(r³/GM). Energy is K = GMm/2r, U = −GMm/r, E = −GMm/2r. Orbital speed is mass-independent; higher orbits are slower but longer-period. Weightlessness arises from continuous free fall.</td></tr><tr><td>13: Motion of a Satellite</td><td>A satellite orbits when gravity supplies the centripetal force: $v_0 = \sqrt{GM/r}$ and $T = 2\pi\sqrt{r^3/GM}$. Energy is $K = \frac{GMm}{2r}$, $U = -\frac{GMm}{r}$, $E = -\frac{GMm}{2r}$. Orbital speed is mass-independent; higher orbits are slower but longer-period. Weightlessness arises from continuous free fall.</td></tr><tr><td>14: Escape Velocity</td><td>Energy conservation gives ve=√(2GM/R)=√(2gR) for an ideal surface launch. It is independent of projectile mass and √2 times circular speed at the same radius.</td></tr><tr><td>15: Geostationary Satellite</td><td>A geostationary satellite orbits in the equatorial plane west-to-east with a 24-hour period, so it appears fixed over one point on the equator. It sits at a unique altitude of 35,800 km (r = 4.22×10⁷ m) and moves at 3.07 km/s. Geostationary orbits are vital for communications and weather satellites.</td></tr><tr><td>16: Potential and Kinetic Energy of the Satellite</td><td>Gravitational potential V = −GM/r is the work per unit mass to bring a test mass from infinity; potential energy U = −GMm/r is the negative binding energy of two masses. Near the surface U ≈ mgh. The field relates to the potential by g = −dV/dr, and circular orbits have total energy E = −GMm/2r, confirming their bound nature.</td></tr><tr><td>17: GPS</td><td>The Global Positioning System uses ≥ 4 satellites whose positions and times are known to compute a receiver's (x, y, z, t) by measuring signal travel times (d = c·Δt). The constellation orbits at ≈ 20,200 km with ≈ 12 h periods, and relativistic clock corrections of ~38 µs/day are essential to maintain metre-level accuracy.</td></tr></table></body></html>